          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get chest discomfort only when I am anxious, never on exertion. How do I tell the difference, and what does guidance describe about assessing it?",
          "answer": "I can't tell you from this information whether your chest discomfort is anxiety-related or heart-related. The guidance I have does not describe a way for you to tell them apart on your own.\n\n**Call emergency services right away** if your chest discomfort comes with any of these: pressure, tightness, squeezing or heaviness lasting more than a few minutes, or coming and going; pain spreading to the arm, shoulder, neck, jaw, back or upper belly; breathlessness, cold sweat, nausea or vomiting, light-headedness, or a sense of doom. This comes from the red-flag chest pain guidance, which treats these signs as a possible heart attack until proven otherwise.\n\nOn how the two can differ, the coronary artery disease reference says that heart-related chest pressure is classically brought on by exertion or emotional stress and eased by rest. That means emotional stress can also trigger heart-related pain, so your pattern of only feeling it when anxious does not rule out a heart cause. The same reference notes that heart symptoms are often not classic, and that they are more easily missed in women, older adults and people with diabetes.\n\nThe GERD reference adds that chest discomfort should not be assumed to be reflux, because heartburn and heart pain cannot be reliably told apart by how they feel.\n\nThe guidance I found does not say how a clinician would assess anxiety-related chest discomfort, and it does not describe any tests. Because you have never had this with exertion, a clinician is the right person to check it. Please raise it with a doctor, particularly if it is new, changing, or happening more often.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
